1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
The last section in the GI pathology chapter

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,340
is colorectal carcinoma.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,780
This is a carcinoma that arises

4
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:08,600
from the colonic or rectal mucosa.

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,700
The peak incidence is about 60 to 70 years of age.

6
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,500
Recall that colorectal carcinoma

7
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:15,440
is the third most common site of cancer

8
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:16,580
when you exclude skin,

9
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,160
and it's also the third most common cause

10
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:20,400
of cancer-related death.

11
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:22,840
Now, when you think about the molecular mechanism

12
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,080
by which colorectal carcinoma can arise,

13
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:27,620
there's really two major molecular pathways.

14
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:31,700
The first molecular pathway is the adenoma carcinoma sequence,

15
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,000
and we discussed the adenoma carcinoma sequence

16
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,360
in extensive detail in the prior lecture.

17
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:39,260
However, this is the most common pathway

18
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,140
by which colorectal carcinoma would arise,

19
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,620
and it probably represents about 80%

20
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,300
of colorectal carcinoma.

21
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,580
There's a second important molecular pathway,

22
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:50,280
and that's the microsatellite instability pathway.

23
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,460
Now, what is this pathway?

24
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:54,540
Well, let's begin by reminding ourselves

25
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:56,360
about what microsatellites are.

26
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,560
So, microsatellites are repeating sequences

27
00:00:58,560 --> 00:00:59,860
of non-coding DNA.

28
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:01,120
They're present in our DNA,

29
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:03,680
and every time we have cell division

30
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:05,200
and we copy our DNA,

31
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:07,420
we also copy the microsatellites.

32
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:09,260
Normally, when we have cell division,

33
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:10,800
we will copy the microsatellites

34
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:12,080
exactly the way they are,

35
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:14,460
and we call that microsatellite stability.

36
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:16,740
Now, let's pretend, for example,

37
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:17,900
or let's assume, for example,

38
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,860
that the patient has some defect

39
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:21,380
in DNA copy mechanisms.

40
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,060
For example, they have a problem

41
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:25,200
with their DNA mismatch repair enzymes.

42
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,800
Well, in that particular case,

43
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,240
they may not be able to maintain

44
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,080
the stability of their microsatellites

45
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,540
as they copy the DNA over and over again,

46
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:33,780
and this would introduce

47
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:34,980
something called instability,

48
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:36,760
and that's exactly what we mean

49
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:39,080
when we say that there is a molecular pathway

50
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:40,700
called microsatellite instability,

51
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:42,760
i.e., there are problems

52
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:45,420
with the DNA copy mechanism

53
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:47,740
so that the microsatellites become unstable.

54
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:48,580
Now, of course,

55
00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:49,920
if the microsatellites are unstable,

56
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:51,460
that's a sign that other genes

57
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:52,660
would also be unstable,

58
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:56,340
and therefore, mutations in other genes

59
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:57,580
could potentially lead

60
00:01:57,580 --> 00:01:59,420
to the development

61
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:01,040
of a colorectal carcinoma.

62
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:03,220
The prototypic example

63
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,560
of this particular pathway,

64
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:07,840
of the microsatellite instability pathway,

65
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,000
is called hereditary

66
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,660
nonpolyposis colorectal carcinoma.

67
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:13,800
Now, hereditary implies

68
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:14,660
that it's an inherited problem,

69
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:16,040
and in fact,

70
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:16,660
it's an inherited mutation

71
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:19,460
in the DNA mismatch repair enzymes.

72
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,660
Nonpolyposis indicates

73
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,060
that the tumors usually arise de novo,

74
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:25,960
meaning you don't get an adenoma

75
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,140
that becomes a carcinoma.

76
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:29,540
You simply get the carcinoma over time.

77
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,340
And then colorectal carcinoma

78
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:32,860
indicates what type of tumor

79
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:33,740
these patients get.

80
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:34,700
They have an increased risk

81
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,900
for colorectal carcinoma.

82
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:36,580
However,

83
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:38,920
because there is an inherited defect

84
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:39,920
in all of the cells,

85
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:41,600
there is also an increased risk

86
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:42,740
in a couple other organs.

87
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:43,660
In particular,

88
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:44,520
it's worth knowing

89
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:46,040
that patients with HNPCC

90
00:02:46,040 --> 00:02:47,740
also have an increased risk

91
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:50,100
of ovarian and endometrial carcinoma.

92
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:51,140
And that's particularly

93
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:52,120
high yield to know

94
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:53,320
that these patients get

95
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,860
basically three types of tumors,

96
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:56,580
colorectal, ovarian,

97
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:58,600
and endometrial carcinomas.

98
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:00,400
So these are the two pathways

99
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,220
by which colon cancer can arise.

100
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:04,800
And it's probably important to note

101
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:05,520
that there's sort of

102
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:07,220
a couple other patients

103
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:08,700
that classically also get

104
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:09,900
colorectal carcinoma

105
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:11,360
that you also want to think about

106
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,080
when you think about pathways, per se.

107
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:14,920
One would be to remember

108
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:17,260
that patients with ulcerative colitis

109
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:18,300
have an increased risk

110
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,740
for colorectal carcinoma,

111
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:21,940
and that sort of occurs independently.

112
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:24,240
It's really the chronic inflammation

113
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:26,100
that leads to the increased risk

114
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:27,320
for development of cancer.

115
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:28,780
And then remember that patients

116
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:29,980
with Putz-Jager syndrome

117
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:31,400
also have an increased risk

118
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,580
for colorectal carcinoma.

119
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:33,880
So there are a few others.

120
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:34,940
However, of course,

121
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,100
the two major pathways

122
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,460
and what represents

123
00:03:37,460 --> 00:03:38,300
the vast majority

124
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:39,760
of colorectal carcinoma

125
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,840
is the adenoma carcinoma sequence.

126
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,220
That's one example.

127
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:44,940
And then the other one

128
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:46,740
would be the microsatellite

129
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:47,760
instability pathway,

130
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,720
the prototypic example

131
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:51,520
of which is HNPCC.

132
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:54,680
Now, screening for colorectal carcinoma,

133
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:55,800
just generally speaking,

134
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:57,420
it occurs via two mechanisms.

135
00:03:57,820 --> 00:03:59,660
Number one is colonoscopy.

136
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:01,560
And I note here

137
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:03,860
that your book actually says endoscopy,

138
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:05,880
and I'm purposely highlighting this for you.

139
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:07,240
It should not say endoscopy.

140
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:08,560
It should say colonoscopy.

141
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,100
So please do correct that.

142
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:11,960
So again, the first mechanism

143
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:13,820
for screening for colorectal carcinoma

144
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:15,500
occurs via colonoscopy.

145
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:16,900
And the second is

146
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:18,120
fecal occult blood testing.

147
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:19,480
Usually we begin screening

148
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:20,360
at the age of 50.

149
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:22,320
The idea for colonoscopy

150
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:24,880
is to remove adenominus polyps.

151
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:26,760
Again, you want to remove all polyps

152
00:04:26,760 --> 00:04:27,460
because you can't tell

153
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:28,640
which ones are adenominus

154
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:29,920
when you do the colonoscopy.

155
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:32,840
However, the goal was

156
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:34,300
to remove the adenominus ones.

157
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:36,600
And so we remove the adenominus polyps

158
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,000
before they can become cancer.

159
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,280
And at the same time,

160
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:40,800
we also try to detect cancer

161
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:42,560
before it presents any clinical symptoms.

162
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,500
So that's the goal of colonoscopy.

163
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:46,300
Fecal occult blood testing

164
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:47,560
does the same thing.

165
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,620
It also has the ability

166
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:49,520
to detect bleeding.

167
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:51,400
And bleeding could be seen with polyps

168
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:53,020
and it could be seen with cancer.

169
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:54,180
So it's a mechanism

170
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:55,180
by which we are, again,

171
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:56,640
trying to detect both polyps

172
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:59,540
and, again, also trying to detect cancer

173
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,680
before clinical symptoms arise.

174
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:04,300
Now, it's important to also note

175
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:05,300
that cancer can develop

176
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:07,560
anywhere along the entire length of the colon.

177
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:09,700
Left-sided carcinoma

178
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,780
usually grows as a napkin ring lesion.

179
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:12,640
And the idea here is

180
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:14,140
that if you've got a left-sided cancer,

181
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:16,160
it tends to grow circumferentially

182
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:17,520
around the entire lumen

183
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:19,440
and it squeezes down on the lumen

184
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:22,580
as if a ring would squeeze on a napkin.

185
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:25,080
And so they call that a napkin ring lesion.

186
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,140
Now, the classical clinical features

187
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:28,420
of such a lesion would be

188
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:29,960
that there would be decreased stool caliber.

189
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:31,020
Now, imagine if the stool

190
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:31,900
is coming through here,

191
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,920
as it gets squeezed to the napkin ring,

192
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:36,580
it will create pencil-thin stools.

193
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:38,720
And so the stool caliber would decrease.

194
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:40,800
There could be left lower quadrant pain.

195
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:43,300
There could also be blood streaking on the stool.

196
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:45,360
But I think the number one thing is here,

197
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,060
the decreased stool caliber.

198
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:49,100
Also important to note

199
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:50,800
that the adenoma carcinoma sequence

200
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,880
tends to be more on the left side

201
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,100
than it is on the right side.

202
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:55,880
At the same time,

203
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:57,860
we can also develop right-sided carcinoma.

204
00:05:58,120 --> 00:05:58,880
And the interesting thing

205
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,460
about right-sided carcinoma

206
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:02,420
is that it tends to grow as a raised lesion.

207
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:03,680
So if this is the right side,

208
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:06,160
it tends to grow as this kind of bump

209
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:07,180
or raised lesion

210
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:09,580
on the surface of the colonic mucosa.

211
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:11,340
Now, again, this isn't a polyp.

212
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,400
It's not arising from an adenomatous polyp.

213
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:14,740
It just simply grows

214
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:16,900
as a raised lesion on the surface.

215
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:20,400
And this would then result

216
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:22,340
in iron deficiency anemia.

217
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:23,720
The right-sided colon

218
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:27,680
has a much larger luminal caliber

219
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,000
than the left side.

220
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:30,840
And so you don't really get obstructive symptoms.

221
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,660
You get bleeding.

222
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:33,300
And in particular,

223
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:35,740
you can see small amounts of bleeding

224
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:37,080
which would slowly over time

225
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:39,140
result in an iron deficiency anemia.

226
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:41,040
And you would also get vague pain.

227
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:43,420
Now, again, right-sided carcinoma

228
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,200
is more classically associated

229
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,620
with the microsatellite instability pathway.

230
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:48,960
Again, important to note,

231
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:51,020
left-sided usually associated

232
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:52,660
with adenoma carcinoma sequence

233
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:54,800
and right-sided usually associated

234
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:57,240
with the microsatellite instability pathway.

235
00:06:57,680 --> 00:06:58,600
Generally speaking,

236
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:00,560
when patients have colorectal carcinoma,

237
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:02,600
there is an overall increased risk

238
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,820
for streptococcus bovis endocarditis

239
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:06,240
so that if you have a patient

240
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:07,740
and they have endocarditis

241
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:09,380
and there's streptococcus bovis

242
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:10,380
growing in the blood,

243
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:11,880
you better really think twice

244
00:07:11,880 --> 00:07:13,200
about colorectal carcinoma.

245
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:14,600
And you can guarantee yourself

246
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:15,720
that this is the type of thing

247
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:17,560
that examiners love to ask about.

248
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:18,960
They give you a history of a patient

249
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:19,840
with endocarditis,

250
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:21,780
they tell you that it's streptococcus bovis,

251
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:22,600
and then they ask you

252
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:23,540
what you need to do next.

253
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:24,760
And of course you need to examine

254
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:25,840
the GI tract,

255
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:26,920
in particular the colon.

256
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:29,620
Now the staging for colorectal carcinoma,

257
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:30,920
the T stage is based

258
00:07:30,920 --> 00:07:31,900
on the depth of invasion.

259
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:33,320
Again, generally speaking,

260
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:34,700
when you have a hollow tube

261
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:36,080
and you have a cancer growing

262
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:36,860
in that hollow tube,

263
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:38,260
depth of invasion becomes

264
00:07:38,260 --> 00:07:39,340
particularly important

265
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:40,440
for that T staging.

266
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:42,040
At the same time,

267
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:43,920
the end stage is based

268
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:45,440
on spread to regional lymph nodes,

269
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,100
as it would be

270
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:47,100
in almost every organ.

271
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:48,640
And then the M,

272
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:49,760
or metastasis,

273
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:51,460
is based on distant spread.

274
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,340
And it's important to note

275
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:54,700
that the most common site

276
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:56,040
of distant metastasis

277
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:57,060
is actually the liver.

278
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:58,840
So that's where colorectal carcinoma

279
00:07:58,840 --> 00:07:59,360
would go

280
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,020
once it leaves the region.

281
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:03,200
An important tumor marker

282
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:04,780
is CEA.

283
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:06,080
Now, remember,

284
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:06,660
tumor markers,

285
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:07,360
generally speaking,

286
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:08,900
can have three possible benefits.

287
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:09,900
You can use tumor markers

288
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:10,460
for screening,

289
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:11,180
you can use them

290
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:12,840
to assess treatment response,

291
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:13,840
and you can also use them

292
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:15,460
to assess or detect recurrence.

293
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:16,880
In this particular case,

294
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:18,800
CEA is not useful for screening.

295
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:20,720
It is useful for detecting,

296
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:21,820
sorry,

297
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:22,720
it is useful

298
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:24,540
for assessing treatment response.

299
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:26,380
If the patient has an elevated CEA

300
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:27,960
at the time of diagnosis,

301
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:29,480
you would look for the CEA

302
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:30,940
to decrease after response.

303
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:31,860
And also,

304
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:33,380
if the patient had an elevated CEA

305
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:34,340
at the time of diagnosis

306
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:35,660
and is then treated

307
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:36,760
and then comes back

308
00:08:36,760 --> 00:08:37,680
with signs and symptoms

309
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:38,900
that are worrisome

310
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:39,920
for recurrence,

311
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:40,820
you could use it

312
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:41,920
to detect recurrence.

313
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:43,440
So these are the two benefits

314
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:44,580
of CEA.

315
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:45,840
And with that,

316
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:46,440
we close off

317
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:48,000
our GI pathology chapter.

